{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791731097,"nonce":"RVdfn/P2hT1e7vxckms8iq+3Vdg=","title":"display: inline-block, inline-flex, inline-grid and inline","message":"The engine reads the inline forms of display (blinc_abi branch inline-display, 2328cca, stacked on ellipsis) and hands the declared keyword to the host. The host's inline flow now places a box by it: inline-block, inline-flex and inline-grid are boxes in the line, whatever their tag, as big as their own layout makes them, on the baseline of their first text, wrapping with the words around them; inline makes any element part of the line; block on a span takes a line. An element that is itself flex or grid is no flow: its children are its items. Tests: native-inline-block (a badge between words, wrapping, a chip with a flex inside, block and inline, taken away again), which fails on the previous inline flow. The wiki's Inline text example has a badge and a chip.\n\nOpen until the engine branch lands: native/Cargo.toml stays at ../../blinc_abi-inline, uncommitted. Do not push before then.","files":null}]}